<!-- Synthetic assembly: read_split saves &amp;obj (rbp-0x18) in a stack pointer,
     stores its argument straight into rbp-0x14, calls write_word(x, p, 0), and
     returns the four bytes read through p. The direct store splits the frame
     map at rbp-0x14, so the four-byte symbol at rbp-0x18 is only a piece of the
     object the program reaches through the address. Declaring that piece as
     int4 would let a callee's wider write run past it; the declaration keeps
     the address width instead.
     Handwritten x86-64 SysV code; no external fixture is required. -->
